@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2216 = private unnamed_addr constant [831 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for dictionary keys that are repeated with different values.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ADictionary keys should be unique. If a key is repeated with a different\0Avalue, the first values will be overwritten and the key will correspond to\0Athe last value. This is likely a mistake.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afoo = {\0A    bar: 1,\0A    baz: 2,\0A    baz: 3,\0A}\0Afoo[baz]  # 3\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afoo = {\0A    bar: 1,\0A    baz: 2,\0A}\0Afoo[baz]  # 2\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe because removing a repeated dictionary key\0Amay delete comments that are attached to the removed key-value pair. This can also change\0Athe program's behavior if the value expressions have side effects.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Dictionaries](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries)\0A", align 1
