@anon.b4276b7b39cc559116fc5d29527aa342.2259 = private unnamed_addr constant [1778 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__post_init__` dataclass methods with parameter defaults.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAdding a default value to a parameter in a `__post_init__` method has no\0Aimpact on whether the parameter will have a default value in the dataclass's\0Agenerated `__init__` method. To create an init-only dataclass parameter with\0Aa default value, you should use an `InitVar` field in the dataclass's class\0Abody and give that `InitVar` field a default value.\0A\0AAs the [documentation] states:\0A\0A> Init-only fields are added as parameters to the generated `__init__()`\0A> method, and are passed to the optional `__post_init__()` method. They are\0A> not otherwise used by dataclasses.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom dataclasses import InitVar, dataclass\0A\0A\0A@dataclass\0Aclass Foo:\0A    bar: InitVar[int] = 0\0A\0A    def __post_init__(self, bar: int = 1, baz: int = 2) -> None:\0A        print(bar, baz)\0A\0A\0Afoo = Foo()  # Prints '0 2'.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom dataclasses import InitVar, dataclass\0A\0A\0A@dataclass\0Aclass Foo:\0A    bar: InitVar[int] = 1\0A    baz: InitVar[int] = 2\0A\0A    def __post_init__(self, bar: int, baz: int) -> None:\0A        print(bar, baz)\0A\0A\0Afoo = Foo()  # Prints '1 2'.\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix is always marked as unsafe because, although switching to `InitVar` is usually correct,\0Ait is incorrect when the parameter is not intended to be part of the public API or when the value\0Ais meant to be shared across all instances.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Post-init processing](https://docs.python.org/3/library/dataclasses.html#post-init-processing)\0A- [Python documentation: Init-only variables](https://docs.python.org/3/library/dataclasses.html#init-only-variables)\0A\0A[documentation]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/dataclasses.html#init-only-variables\0A", align 1
